+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSocket delivery of committed revisions
+
+## Context
+
+Each committed edit on an event board receives one authoritative event revision, assigned server-side when an editor's HTTPS command is accepted. How that revision reaches browsers was open when the board's architecture was set. The board serves up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers across 40 weekend markets; a busy market averages two status changes per minute, with bursts of 20 edits per second around opening time. A browser that loses connectivity must be brought back to the current board after being away for up to 20 minutes.
+
+Every candidate route meets the project's freshness goal of showing committed changes within 15 seconds, and every candidate can carry 800 connected browsers. The choice is therefore not about whether the board works — it is about where the work of keeping browsers current lands, and about how much of the board's update path the market wants to hand to a third party.
+
+The project tenet "Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted" governs the board's infrastructure as a whole, so managed infrastructure remained an option here. This decision scopes that permission more narrowly for one path only: the update path from a committed revision to connected browsers.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **The update path must not require the market to buy realtime service capacity from a third party.** Doc-local gate, set by this decision: it defines the option set for delivery and the reason a hosted realtime service is not a candidate. Polling and self-hosted sockets both pass; a hosted realtime service is eliminated on this gate rather than merely scored down.
+
+### Weighted criteria
+
+- **Update latency** — major. How long a committed revision takes to appear in a connected browser, and whether that delay grows with load. The project wants updates to feel immediate rather than merely to arrive inside the 15-second goal.
+- **Load shape during bursts** — moderate. How the cost of delivering one revision scales when a market's edit rate rises, and whether a burst multiplies read traffic.
+- **Operational work carried by the market** — moderate. The connection, session, reconnection, and publication machinery the market must operate and keep healthy.
+- **Repeated read load on the change store** — minor. The steady-state read volume the board's own store must serve to keep browsers current.
+- **Independence from a paid realtime vendor** — major. How exposed the board is to a third party's subscription model, connection pricing, and provider-specific constraints on how the board may behave.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers re-request the board over ordinary HTTPS on a timer, roughly every five seconds, and the server answers each request with the current board state or the changes since the browser's last read. Nothing about a request implies a long-lived connection: every poll is independent, and the server holds no session state between them. The board's own HTTPS endpoint carries both the read traffic and the command traffic.
+
+**Demonstration.** 800 browsers polling a five-second interval issue roughly 160 board reads per second against the store at steady state. An opening-time burst of 20 edits per second does not reduce that read rate; it adds to the write side of the same store while the read side continues.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **weak.** A change is visible only when the next poll fires, so browsers see it up to five seconds later — inside the goal, but a visible lag behind a board that has already committed the change.
+- Load shape during bursts: **weak.** Bursts multiply write work on the store while the read rate stays high regardless, so the store serves both a heavy read stream and a heavy write stream at the same moment.
+- Operational work carried by the market: **strong.** There are no connections to capacity-plan, no sessions to expire, no reconnection logic, and no publication machinery. The work is the ordinary request path the board already has.
+- Repeated read load on the change store: **weak.** Every browser reads on a timer, so read volume is a function of browser count and poll interval rather than of edit rate — the store's read cost is paid continuously, including when nothing has changed.
+- Independence from a paid realtime vendor: **strong.** The board reads its own endpoint; no provider pricing or provider model applies.
+
+**Summary.** The simplest possible delivery, trading immediacy and continuous read load for the absence of any connection machinery.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The market runs a WebSocket service that browsers hold a connection to. Each committed revision is published by the change store and pushed down those connections as it is committed, rather than waiting for a browser to ask for it. A browser that loses its connection reconnects and asks for the revisions it missed, resuming from the last revision it saw. The board keeps the revision history that makes this replay possible and a publication path that hands each committed revision to the delivery service exactly once and in revision order.
+
+**Demonstration.** A market's 800 browsers hold 800 socket sessions. Each committed revision is published once and travels to the connected sessions; a browser that was disconnected for ten minutes reconnects and requests the revisions since its last-seen revision, which the store serves from retained history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **strong.** A revision reaches connected browsers as it is committed, with no polling interval between the commit and the display.
+- Load shape during bursts: **strong.** The cost of a burst is one publication per revision; fan-out to connected browsers does not add store reads, so the store's read load stays flat while edits accelerate.
+- Operational work carried by the market: **partial.** The market owns socket capacity for connected browsers, session lifecycle, reconnection and catch-up in the browser, replayable revisions in the store, and reliable publication from store to delivery. This is the heaviest of the three in machinery to operate.
+- Repeated read load on the change store: **strong.** Reads follow edits — one publication per revision plus catch-up reads on reconnect — instead of a constant stream from every browser.
+- Independence from a paid realtime vendor: **strong.** The board owns the connection path outright; no provider pricing, capacity, or subscription model applies.
+
+**Summary.** Immediate updates on infrastructure the market owns, paid for in connection and reconnect machinery.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime provider runs the connection layer. The board publishes each committed revision to the provider, and the provider fans it out to the browsers subscribed to the relevant event through the provider's own client library and subscription model. The market operates the store and the publication step, but the provider operates connections, capacity, and reconnection.
+
+**Demonstration.** The market pays the provider per active connection or per message on top of a base subscription. A provider-side incident or a change to the provider's subscription model becomes the board's incident.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **strong.** A revision reaches connected browsers as it is committed, through the provider's network path.
+- Load shape during bursts: **strong.** The provider absorbs the fan-out, so the store's read load stays flat while edits accelerate.
+- Operational work carried by the market: **strong.** The provider handles connections, capacity, and reconnection; the market's remaining work is publication to the provider and reconciling what the provider confirms it received.
+- Repeated read load on the change store: **strong.** Reads follow edits rather than browser count.
+- Independence from a paid realtime vendor: **weak.** The board's update path sits behind the provider's subscription model, connection pricing, and client library, and a provider incident becomes the board's incident.
+
+**Summary.** Immediate updates with the least connection work, at the price of a recurring vendor relationship on the update path.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets are chosen.
+
+The gate settles one option first: a hosted realtime service is eliminated because it requires the market to buy realtime capacity from a third party, which this decision does not permit on the update path. The remaining contest is between five-second polling and self-hosted WebSockets, and both pass the gate, so the choice turns on the weighted criteria.
+
+Update latency, a major criterion, separates them. Polling satisfies the 15-second freshness goal, but a committed change would sit unseen in browsers for up to five seconds, which the project does not want to spend its freshness budget on. Self-hosted WebSockets deliver the revision as it is committed. Read load and burst behaviour point the same way: polling's cost is a function of browser count and timer interval, so the store serves heavy reads continuously and simultaneously with burst writes, whereas sockets publish once per revision and read only for catch-up. Self-hosted WebSockets are strong on both; polling is weak on both.
+
+The cost that was accepted is operational work — the heaviest of the three options — and it is accepted deliberately: socket capacity for connected browsers, session lifecycle in delivery, reconnection and catch-up in the browser, replayable revisions and reliable publication in the change store. The alternative to carrying that work was carrying a vendor relationship on the update path instead, which the gate rules out, or a five-second lag and a constant read stream, which the criteria weigh against the project's stated preference for updates that feel immediate.
+
+Independence from a paid realtime vendor, the second major criterion, is satisfied by the chosen option outright: the board owns its connection path, and no provider pricing, capacity limit, or subscription model applies to how the board behaves.
+
+What this decision commits the project to: the change store must retain revisions for replay and must publish each committed revision reliably; delivery must own socket sessions and their resumption points; the browser side must own reconnection and catch-up. What it forecloses: a hosted realtime service, for as long as the gate stands, and any move that would put the board's update path behind a provider's model without reopening this decision.
